.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-INVENTORY 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-inventory
.Nd Ansible inventory of the kldload estate, read from the state database
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -list | Fl -host Ar host | Fl -dump
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an Ansible dynamic inventory script.
It reads the kldload state database, which
.Xr kldload-db 8
keeps, and prints every machine and cluster node that has an address,
grouped so that plays can name them:
.Bl -tag -width "kube_control_plane"
.It Li all
Every host.
.It Li kube
.Li kube_control_plane
and
.Li kube_workers .
.It Li cluster_ Ns Ar name
The members of a cluster.
.It Li role_ Ns Ar role
By role:
.Li role_control_plane , role_worker , role_clone ,
and so on.
.It Li status_ Ns Ar status
By state:
.Li status_running , status_draining ,
and so on.
.It Li ungrouped
Hosts in no cluster.
.El
.Pp
Group names use
.Ql _
where the value had
.Ql - ,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&:
or a space; Ansible reads a
.Ql \&:
in a group name as two patterns.
.Pp
Each host's
.Li ansible_host
is its management WireGuard address when it has one, then its second
WireGuard address, then its address on the libvirt network, so plays go
over the encrypted mesh whenever it is up.
.Li ansible_user
is root, and the key is
.Pa /root/.ssh/id_ed25519
when the reader can see it.
The host's kldload role, cluster, state and mesh addresses or id travel as
.Li kldload_*
variables.
.Pp
A machine whose address was cleared \(em
.Xr kldload-networks 8
does that when its domain is not running \(em and a node marked offline
or deleted are left out: an address that DHCP has given to another
machine must not be a target.
.Pp
kldload's Ansible configuration names this script as its inventory, so
.Xr ansible 1
needs no
.Fl i .
The database is opened read-only; a member of the
.Ql kldload
group can run it.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -list
The whole inventory as one line of JSON, with every host's variables
under
.Li _meta ;
also what it prints with no option.
.It Fl -host Ar host
One host's variables;
.Ql {}
for a host it does not have.
.It Fl -dump
The whole inventory, indented, for reading.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_DB
The database; default
.Pa /var/lib/kldload/state.db .
.It Ev KLDLOAD_SSH_KEY , Ev KLDLOAD_SSH_USER
The key and user given to every host.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/lib/kldload/state.db
The state database.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Printed.
A database that does not exist is an empty inventory, not an error.
.It 1
An error, named on standard error; an empty inventory is printed all the
same, so Ansible still reads JSON.
.It 2
A usage error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload host on 2026-09-29, against the demonstration
database built in the examples of
.Xr kldload-db 8
.Pq Ev KLDLOAD_DB Ns = Ns Pa /var/tmp/dbdemo/state.db :
a cluster
.Ql lab
with a control plane registered as a node and a worker registered as a
machine.
.Ss As Ansible sees it
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ansible-inventory -i /usr/local/bin/kldload-inventory --graph
@all:
  |--@ungrouped:
  |--@kube:
  |  |--@kube_control_plane:
  |  |  |--lab-cp-1
  |  |--@kube_workers:
  |  |  |--lab-w-1
  |--@role_control_plane:
  |  |--lab-cp-1
  |--@status_draining:
  |  |--lab-cp-1
  |--@cluster_lab:
  |  |--lab-cp-1
  |  |--lab-w-1
  |--@role_worker:
  |  |--lab-w-1
  |--@status_running:
  |  |--lab-w-1
# (exited 0)
$ ansible -i /usr/local/bin/kldload-inventory kube_workers --list-hosts
  hosts (1):
    lab-w-1
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss One host (--host)
The control plane is reached over the mesh, the worker, with no mesh
address recorded, over the libvirt network:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-inventory --host lab-cp-1
{"ansible_host": "10.250.0.21", "ansible_user": "root", "kldload_role": "control_plane", "kldload_cluster": "lab", "kldload_status": "draining", "kldload_wg_ips": {"wg0": "10.250.0.21", "wg1": "", "wg2": "", "wg3": ""}, "kldload_facts": {}}
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-inventory --host lab-w-1
{"ansible_host": "192.168.122.33", "ansible_user": "root", "kldload_role": "worker", "kldload_cluster": "lab", "kldload_status": "running", "kldload_mesh_id": ""}
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-inventory --host no-such-host
{}
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Everything (--list)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-inventory --list
{"all": {"hosts": ["lab-cp-1", "lab-w-1"], "vars": {}}, "ungrouped": {"hosts": []}, "kube": {"children": ["kube_control_plane", "kube_workers"]}, "kube_control_plane": {"hosts": ["lab-cp-1"]}, "kube_workers": {"hosts": ["lab-w-1"]}, "_meta": {"hostvars": {"lab-cp-1": {"ansible_host": "10.250.0.21", "ansible_user": "root", "kldload_role": "control_plane", "kldload_cluster": "lab", "kldload_status": "draining", "kldload_wg_ips": {"wg0": "10.250.0.21", "wg1": "", "wg2": "", "wg3": ""}, "kldload_facts": {}}, "lab-w-1": {"ansible_host": "192.168.122.32", "ansible_user": "root", "kldload_role": "worker", "kldload_cluster": "lab", "kldload_status": "running", "kldload_mesh_id": "31"}}}, "role_control_plane": {"hosts": ["lab-cp-1"]}, "status_draining": {"hosts": ["lab-cp-1"]}, "cluster_lab": {"hosts": ["lab-cp-1", "lab-w-1"]}, "role_worker": {"hosts": ["lab-w-1"]}, "status_running": {"hosts": ["lab-w-1"]}}
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
That was printed before the worker was deleted and registered again,
with a new address and no mesh id.
.Ss No database
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ KLDLOAD_DB=/nonexistent kldload-inventory --list
{"all": {"hosts": [], "vars": {}}, "ungrouped": {"hosts": []}, "kube": {"children": ["kube_control_plane", "kube_workers"]}, "kube_control_plane": {"hosts": []}, "kube_workers": {"hosts": []}, "_meta": {"hostvars": {}}}
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss For reading (--dump)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-inventory --dump
{
  "_meta": {
    "hostvars": {
      "lab-cp-1": {
        "ansible_host": "10.250.0.21",
        "ansible_user": "root",
        "kldload_cluster": "lab",
        "kldload_facts": {},
        "kldload_role": "control_plane",
        "kldload_status": "draining",
        "kldload_wg_ips": {
          "wg0": "10.250.0.21",
          "wg1": "",
          "wg2": "",
          "wg3": ""
        }
      },
      "lab-w-1": {
        "ansible_host": "192.168.122.33",
        "ansible_user": "root",
        "kldload_cluster": "lab",
        "kldload_mesh_id": "",
        "kldload_role": "worker",
        "kldload_status": "running"
      }
    }
  },
\&...
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "kldload-inventory error: MESSAGE"
Anything that stopped it; the inventory printed is empty and the exit
status is 1.
.It "kldload-inventory: PATH: MESSAGE; reading without its WAL -- the newest changes may be missing"
The database could not be opened read-only as it is \(em not seen in
practice; a member of the
.Ql kldload
group, and a user outside it, both read it normally.
The inventory is then read without the changes not yet written back.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ansible 1 ,
.Xr ansible-inventory 1 ,
.Xr kldload-db 8 ,
.Xr kldload-estate 8 ,
.Xr kldload-networks 8
.Sh HISTORY
Every
.Ql role: ,
.Ql status:
and
.Ql cluster:
group was once untargetable, Ansible reading the colon as a pattern
separator with only a warning; they use
.Ql _
since.
On 2026-08-30 two entries on onyx claimed one address, a stopped machine's
and the one DHCP had since given it; nodes marked offline are left out
since.
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: the database was opened in a way that
skipped its write-ahead log, so everything committed since the last
checkpoint was missing from the inventory; and
.Fl -host
printed
.Ql {}
for every host.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
The inventory is what the database says.
A machine made outside the kldload tools is not in it until it is
registered with
.Xr kldload-db 8 .
